---
import CaseLayout from '../../layouts/CaseLayout.astro';
import { caseStudies } from '../../lib/projects';
import { url } from '../../lib/url';

const project = caseStudies[3];

const sections = [
  { id: 'overview', label: 'Overview' },
  { id: 'docking', label: 'Electromagnetic docking' },
  { id: 'drive', label: 'Making three robots drive straight' },
  { id: 'choreography', label: 'The choreography (in progress)' },
  { id: 'status', label: 'Status, honestly' },
];
---
<CaseLayout project={project} sections={sections} prev={{ slug: 'evoting', title: 'Biometric e-voting' }} next={{ slug: 'crynet', title: 'CryNet' }}>
  <!-- 01 overview -->
  <section class="cs-section" id="overview">
    <div class="rail" data-reveal><span class="rail__num">01</span><span class="rail__label">Overview</span><span class="rail__line"></span></div>
    <p class="body" data-reveal>
      Three ESP32 robots built to <strong>dock, latch and move as one payload</strong>. Each robot
      carries a downward-facing electromagnet; to move an object heavier than any one of them, the
      swarm docks around it, energises its magnets, and drives in formation. It is the simplest
      mechanical answer to "what if the thing is heavier than the robot": no grippers, no arms,
      just coupling.
    </p>
    <dl class="kv" data-reveal>
      <dt>Fleet</dt><dd>3 × ESP32 robots, independent drive electronics per unit</dd>
      <dt>Drive</dt><dd>L298N dual H-bridges, logic-level MOSFETs switching the electromagnets</dd>
      <dt>Power</dt><dd>Buck converters stepping the pack down per unit: one regulator per robot, not one shared rail</dd>
      <dt>Coordination</dt><dd>Python-side path planning against ArUco markers (in progress)</dd>
    </dl>
    <figure class="figure figure--wide" data-reveal>
      <div class="figure__frame"><img src={url('/img/swarm/one-robot.jpg')} alt="One swarm robot from above: ESP32, L298N, buck converter, 18650 cells and the electromagnet coil on a plywood deck" loading="lazy" /></div>
      <figcaption>ONE ROBOT, TOP-DOWN · ESP32, L298N, buck, cells and the coil on the plywood deck</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <!-- 02 docking -->
  <section class="cs-section" id="docking">
    <div class="rail" data-reveal><span class="rail__num">02</span><span class="rail__label">Electromagnetic docking</span><span class="rail__line"></span></div>
    <p class="body" data-reveal>
      The electromagnet is the interesting actuator precisely because it is a hostile load: a bare
      coil driven by a MOSFET fights every switch-off with a flyback spike that will kill an
      unprotected transistor in a week.
    </p>
    <ul class="body bullets" data-reveal>
      <li><strong>Flyback protection on every coil.</strong> Freewheeling diodes across each electromagnet give the collapse current somewhere to go; the drivers survived the whole campaign of dock/latch/release cycles.</li>
      <li><strong>Logic-level MOSFETs as the coil switches.</strong> The ESP32's 3.3 V GPIO drives the gates directly, no gate-driver stage, because the parts were chosen for V<sub>GS(th)</sub> and R<sub>DS(on)</sub> at 3.3 V, not grabbed from a drawer.</li>
      <li><strong>Buck converters per unit.</strong> Motors and coils share nothing electrical with a neighbour: one robot browning out cannot take the formation with it.</li>
    </ul>
    <figure class="figure" data-reveal>
      <div class="figure__frame"><img src={url('/img/swarm/fleet.jpg')} alt="Three swarm robot decks mid-build, L298N drivers mounted on plywood chassis" loading="lazy" /></div>
      <figcaption>THE FLEET, MID-BUILD · three decks, drivers and wheels on</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <!-- 03 drive -->
  <section class="cs-section" id="drive">
    <div class="rail" data-reveal><span class="rail__num">03</span><span class="rail__label">Making three robots drive straight</span><span class="rail__line"></span></div>
    <p class="body" data-reveal>
      Cheap gearmotors differ. Give three robots the same PWM duty and you get three different
      speeds and three gentle curves, which is fatal for a fleet that has to arrive at a dock
      together. So calibration came before choreography.
    </p>
    <div class="note" data-reveal>
      <p class="note__title">Method</p>
      <p>Each robot ran straight-line trials at fixed duty, the drift was measured, and
      <strong>per-wheel PWM compensation was tuned against a marked reference line</strong> until
      each robot tracked true. The fleet's heading error dropped to the point where docking
      alignment became a mechanical concern instead of an electrical one.</p>
    </div>
    <p class="body" data-reveal>
      It is the least glamorous work on this page and some of the most transferable: measure the
      component, not the datasheet's version of it.
    </p>
    <figure class="figure figure--wide" data-reveal>
      <div class="figure__frame">
        <video src={url('/img/swarm/test-drive.mp4')} poster={url('/img/swarm/test-drive-poster.jpg')} autoplay muted loop playsinline></video>
      </div>
      <figcaption>THE STRAIGHT-LINE TRIALS · a unit running the marked line outdoors, per-wheel PWM compensation doing its job</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <!-- 04 choreography -->
  <section class="cs-section" id="choreography">
    <div class="rail" data-reveal><span class="rail__num">04</span><span class="rail__label">The choreography (in progress)</span><span class="rail__line"></span></div>
    <p class="body" data-reveal>
      The upper layer, where the swarm <em>decides</em>, is being built: a Python dashboard that
      watches the arena through a camera, reads ArUco markers, and plans dock-and-carry sequences.
    </p>
    <dl class="kv" data-reveal>
      <dt>Markers</dt><dd>3 payload markers + 3 destination markers: nine unique IDs across the fleet and the arena, so every robot knows which payload is its assignment.</dd>
      <dt>Planner</dt><dd>Dashboard-driven path planning in Python: camera → marker poses → assignment → per-robot waypoints.</dd>
      <dt>State</dt><dd>Robots dock, latch and drive as a formation; coordinated payload transport under the planner is the part still being finished.</dd>
    </dl>
    <div class="note note--amber" data-reveal>
      <p class="note__title">Straight answer</p>
      <p>This build is <strong>not finished</strong>. The drive calibration, the magnet switching
      and the docking mechanics work on the bench; the marker-driven choreography is the open
      chapter. I would rather show the honest state than quietly retire the project. The lab page
      records both what works and what does not yet.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 05 status -->
  <section class="cs-section" id="status">
    <div class="rail" data-reveal><span class="rail__num">05</span><span class="rail__label">Status, honestly</span><span class="rail__line"></span></div>
    <div class="stats" data-reveal>
      <div class="stat"><p class="stat__value tnum">3</p><p class="stat__label">Robots built, each with independent drive + magnet electronics</p></div>
      <div class="stat stat--green"><p class="stat__value tnum">±straight</p><p class="stat__label">Per-wheel PWM calibration against a marked line</p></div>
      <div class="stat"><p class="stat__value tnum">9</p><p class="stat__label">Unique ArUco IDs across payload and destination markers</p></div>
      <div class="stat stat--amber"><p class="stat__value">open</p><p class="stat__label">Planner-driven formation transport, the chapter in progress</p></div>
    </div>
    <p class="body" data-reveal>
      What stands: three working robots, protected electromagnetic coupling, calibrated formation
      driving. What remains: closing the loop from dashboard plan to dock-and-carry.
    </p>
    <figure class="figure" data-reveal>
      <div class="figure__frame"><img src={url('/img/swarm/pwm-test.jpg')} alt="Me running PWM tests on a swarm robot held in both hands, the L298N driver wired on its deck" loading="lazy" /></div>
      <figcaption>THE PWM TESTS · a unit in hand, stepping through the duty range</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>
</CaseLayout>

<style>
  .bullets { margin-top: 1.25rem; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.8rem; }
  .bullets li { padding-left: 1.1rem; position: relative; color: var(--muted); }
  .bullets li::before { content: '▪'; position: absolute; left: 0; color: var(--blue); }
  .bullets :global(strong) { color: var(--text); font-weight: 500; }
  .note { margin-top: 1.5rem; }
</style>
